## 5. On-Page and Technical SEO Checklist (Every Article)

Writing the article is only half the battle. Each post should follow this template to maximize ranking potential:

1. **Title tag formula:** `[Main Keyword] + [Year] + [Hook]` — e.g., "Transport Service in Durgapur: Rates, Routes & Booking Guide (2026)".
2. **Word count:** 1,500–2,500 for guides, 1,000–1,500 for tips posts. Thin content does not rank in logistics.
3. **FAQ section + FAQPage schema markup** at the bottom of every article — this is how you win Google's "People Also Ask" boxes [7].
4. **Comparison tables and rate tables** (your existing rate-guide posts already do this well — keep the format consistent).
5. **Internal linking:** every article must link to 2–3 related LoadSaathi pages (fare calculator, city guide, relevant solution page) and receive back-links from them.
6. **Call-to-action:** shipper articles → "Post a load"; trucker articles → "Register your truck"; rate articles → "Get an instant AI quote" using your fare calculator widget.
7. **Publish cadence:** 2–3 articles per week minimum. Logistics SEO compounds — the Truckstop case study shows that consistent output (50% monthly increase) plus keyword mapping produced a 155% organic traffic increase in 12 months [7].
8. **Freshness:** update every rate guide quarterly with current rates; update e-Way Bill and GST posts whenever GSTN issues an advisory. Google rewards refreshed, accurate compliance content.
9. **Backlinks:** pitch your East India data articles to regional industry publications, trade associations (truck unions, MSME bodies), and logistics newsletters — a backlink from an industry outlet is worth far more than generic guest posts [7].

